/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The tips of the wire are open circuits, so the current must be zero there.
   - I(z) = I₀ cos(βz), −λ/4 ≤ z ≤ λ/4.
   - The current is a cosine half-arch, fuller than the triangular current of a short dipole, so the half-wave dipole radiates far more for the same feed current.
   - An exactly half-wave thin dipole has input impedance Zin = 73 + j42.5 Ω.
   - Adding the fields of Hertzian elements carrying I(z), each with its own phase, gives the far field
   - Eθ = j 60 I₀ e−jβr cos((π/2) cos θ) / (r sin θ).
   - Broadside the magnitude is simply |E| = 60 I₀/r.
   - Integrating the power gives Prad = ½ I₀² × 73.1 W, so Rr = 73 Ω at the feed.
   - Stand one half of the dipole, λ/4 long, on a large conducting sheet and feed it against the sheet.
   - Real ground planes are finite (mast radials, a car roof, a phone’s circuit board), so the impedance and gain move away from these ideals.
*/
