/* book Worked example 2: Q: An AM radio has a 60-turn coil on a ferrite rod 10 mm in diameter with μeff = 80, tuned to 1 MHz. The station gives a field of 1 mV/m at the radio. Find the effective length of the rod antenna and the open-circuit voltage induced in the coil, and its radiation resistance.
   1. λ = 3 × 10⁸ / 10⁶ = 300 m. Area of one turn: A = π × 0.005² = 7.854 × 10⁻⁵ m².
   2. leff = 2π N μeff A/λ = 2π × 60 × 80 × 7.854 × 10⁻⁵ / 300 = 7.90 × 10⁻³ m, about 8 mm.
   3. Voc = E leff = 10⁻³ × 7.90 × 10⁻³ = 7.9 × 10⁻⁶ V = 7.9 µV (rod broadside to the station).
   4. Rr = 31 171 × (N μeff A/λ²)² = 31 171 × (60 × 80 × 7.854 × 10⁻⁵ / 90 000)² = 31 171 × (4.19 × 10⁻⁶)² = 5.5 × 10⁻⁷ Ω.
   5. The coil’s own resistance is ohms, so ηrad is around 10⁻⁷: hopeless as a transmitter, yet the 7.9 µV, raised by the Q of the tuned circuit, is plenty for the receiver.
   Answer: leff ≈ 7.9 mm; Voc ≈ 7.9 µV; Rr ≈ 5.5 × 10⁻⁷ Ω. */
